      "text": "Let the two numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). We can set up a system of equations based on the given conditions. From the problem, we have:\n\n\\[\nx + y = 6 \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = 12 \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRecall that the difference of squares formula can be factored as \\( (x + y)(x - y) = 12 \\). We'll use this formula first.\n\nSubstitute \\( x + y \\) from equation (1) into the difference of squares:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 6:\n\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx - y = 2\n\\]\n\nThis step leads us to a contradiction, so let’s recheck our approach. The correct approach involves solving both equations simultaneously. First, rewrite equation (2):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = 12\n\\]\n\nUsing the difference of squares formula again:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nAnd as before, substitute \\( x + y \\):\n\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nThen solve for \\( x - y \\):\n\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx - y = 2\n\\]\n\nHowever, we should instead solve for each variable since they are both non-negative. Recall equation (1):\n\n\\[\nx + y = 6\n\\]\n\nSubtract the second equation (3) from the first (1) to eliminate \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n(x + y) - (x - y) = 6 - 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2y = 4\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = 2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( y = 2 \\) back into \\( x + y = 6 \\):\n\n\\[\nx + 2 = 6\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nx = 4\n\\]\n\nThe positive difference between \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) is:\n\n\\[\n|x - y| = |4 - 2| = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\boxed{2}.",
